 Pearson correlation coefficients\nNow I\'ll calculate the Pearson correlation matrix for the selected numerical variables.\n```\n```python\n# Calculate Pearson correlation matrix\ncorrelation_matrix = corr_df.corr(method=\'pearson\')\n\n# Display the correlation matrix\nprint("Pearson Correlation Matrix:")\nprint(correlation_matrix)\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 4: Find the strongest positive correlation pair\nI need to identify the pair with the strongest positive correlation (excluding self-correlations).\n```\n```python\n# Convert correlation matrix to a list of pairs and their coefficients\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Get upper triangle of the correlation matrix (excluding diagonal)\nupper_tri = correlation_matrix.where(np.triu(np.ones(correlation_matrix.shape), k=1).astype(bool))\n\n# Create a list of all pairs and their correlation coefficients\npairs = []\nfor col1 in correlation_matrix.columns:\n    for col2 in correlation_matrix.columns:\n        if col1 < col2:  # Only get unique pairs (upper triangle)\n            pairs.append({\n                \'pair\': [col1, col2],\n                \'coefficient\': correlation_matrix.loc[col1, col2]\n            })\n\n# Create a DataFrame for easier manipulation\npairs_df = pd.DataFrame(pairs)\n\n# Find the pair with the strongest positive correlation\nstrongest_pair = pairs_df.loc[pairs_df[\'coefficient\'].idxmax()]\n\nprint("\\nAll correlation pairs:")\nprint(pairs_df.sort_values(\'coefficient\', ascending=False))\n\nprint("\\nStrongest positive correlation pair:")\nprint(f"Pair: {strongest_pair[\'pair\']}")\nprint(f"Coefficient: {strongest_pair[\'coefficient\']}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 5: Format the output according to the required format\nI need to format the output as specified:\n- @strongest_correlation_pair[pair]\n- @strongest_correlation_coefficient[coefficient]\n```\n```python\n# Round the coefficient to 2 decimal places\nstrongest_coefficient = round(strongest_pair[\'coefficient\'], 2)\nstrongest_pair_list = st