ws, 11 columns)\n- **Duration Calculation**: Parsed from `dates_active` column, handling cross-month date ranges\n- **Damage Threshold**: Median damage = $75,001.00\n- **Correlation Method**: Pearson correlation coefficient with two-tailed test (α = 0.05)\n- **Valid Sample Size**: 660 storms (after removing rows with missing duration or damage values)\n\n## Results Summary\n\n### High Damage Group (221 storms, damage > $75,001)\n- **Correlation Coefficient (r)**: 0.61\n- **P-value**: 0.0000\n- **Relationship Type**: Linear\n\n### Low Damage Group (439 storms, damage ≤ $75,001)\n- **Correlation Coefficient (r)**: 0.63\n- **P-value**: 0.0000\n- **Relationship Type**: Linear\n\n## Interpretation\nBoth high and low damage storms show a **significant positive linear relationship** between maximum storm category and duration of activity. This means:\n- Higher category storms tend to last longer\n- The relationship is stronger for low damage storms (r = 0.63) compared to high damage storms (r = 0.61)\n- Both relationships are statistically significant (p < 0.05)\n\n## Key Findings\n1. **Strong Linear Relationship**: Both groups show r ≥ 0.4 with p < 0.05, confirming linear relationships\n2. **Similar Patterns**: The correlation coefficients are very similar (0.61 vs 0.63), suggesting the relationship between storm category and duration is consistent regardless of damage level\n3. **Statistical Significance**: Both correlations are highly significant (p ≈ 0.0000)\n```\n```markdown\n{\n  "final_answer_block": {\n    "question_id": 431,\n    "answers": {\n      "high_damage_correlation_coefficient": 0.61,\n      "high_damage_p_value": 0.0,\n      "high_damage_relationship_type": "linear",\n      "low_damage_correlation_coefficient": 0.63,\n      "low_damage_p_value": 0.0,\n      "low_damage_relationship_type": "linear"\n    },\n    "format_targets": {\n      "high_damage_correlation_coefficient": "@high_damage_correlation_coefficient[0.61]",\n      "high_damage_p_value": "@hi