se the duration from dates_active column, and calculate the median damage to split storms into high and low damage groups.\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 1: Data Loading and Initial Exploration\nI need to:\n1. Load the CSV file\n2. Parse the dates_active column to calculate storm duration\n3. Calculate median damage to create high/low damage groups\n4. Prepare for correlation analysis\n```\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\n# Load the data\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'./input/cost_data_with_errors.csv\')\nprint(f"Original shape: {df.shape}")\nprint(f"\\nColumns: {df.columns.tolist()}")\nprint(f"\\nFirst few rows:\\n{df.head()}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 2: Parse Duration from dates_active Column\nThe dates_active column contains strings like \'April 19 – 21\'. I need to extract the start and end dates to calculate the duration of each storm.\n```\n```python\n# Parse dates_active to calculate duration\ndef parse_duration(date_str):\n    """Parse date string and return duration in days"""\n    if pd.isna(date_str):\n        return np.nan\n    \n    # Handle various date formats\n    try:\n        # Split by \'–\' or \'-\' to get start and end dates\n        parts = str(date_str).split(\'–\')\n        if len(parts) == 2:\n            start_str = parts[0].strip()\n            end_str = parts[1].strip()\n            \n            # Extract day numbers (assuming same month/year for simplicity)\n            import re\n            start_days = re.findall(r\'\\d+\', start_str)\n            end_days = re.findall(r\'\\d+\', end_str)\n            \n            if start_days and end_days:\n                start_day = int(start_days[0])\n                end_day = int(end_days[0])\n                duration = end_day - start_day + 1  # +1 to include both days\n                return duration\n    except:\n        pass\n    return np.nan\n\n# Apply the parsing function\ndf[\'duration\'] = df[\'dates_active\'].apply(parse_duration)\nprint(f"Duration column created. Shape: {df.shape}")\nprint(f"\\nDuration sample:\\n{df[[\'dates_active\', \'duration\']].head(10)}")\nprint(f"\\nDuration missing values: {df[\'duration\'].isna().sum()}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 3: Calculate Median Damage and Split into High/Low Groups\nNow I\'ll calculate the median damage and create binary groups for high and low damage storms.\n```\n```python\n# Calculate median damage\nmedian_damage = df[\'damage_USD\'].median()\nprint(f"Median damage: ${median_damage:,.2f}")\n\n# Create high/low damage groups\ndf[\'damage_group\'] = df[\'damage_USD\'].apply(lambda x: \'high\' if x > median_damage else \'low\')\n\n# Count storms in each group\ngroup_counts = df[\'damage_group\'].value_counts()\nprint(f"\\nStorms by damage group:\\n{group_counts}")\n\n# Check for any NaN in damage_USD\nprint(f"\\nMissing values in damage_USD: {df[\'damage_USD\'].isna().sum()}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 4: Prepare Data for Correlation Analysis\nI need to ensure both max_storm_cat and duration have no missing values for correlation analysis.\n```\n```python\n# Filter rows with valid data for correlation\ndf_valid = df.dropna(subset=[\'max_storm_cat\', \'duration\', \'damage_USD\'])\nprint(f"Valid data shape after dropping NaN: {df_valid.shape}")\n\n# Split into high and low damage groups\nhigh_damage_df = df_valid[df_valid[\'damage_group\'] == \'high\']\nlow_damage_df = df_valid[df_valid[\'damage_group\'] == \'low\']\n\nprint(f"\\nHigh damage storms: {len(high_damage_df)}")\nprint(f"Low damage storms: {len(low_damage_df)}")\n\n# Check max_storm_cat distribution\nprint(f"\\nMax storm category distribution:\\n{df_valid[\'max_storm_cat\'].value_counts().sort_index()}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 5: Calculate Pearson Correlation for Each Group\nNow I\'ll calculate the Pearson correlation coefficient and p-value for both high and low damage groups.\n```\n```python\n# Function to calculate correlation and determine relationship type\ndef calculate_correlation_analysis(df_group, group_name):\n    """Calculate Pearson correlation and determine relationship type"""\n    if len(df_group) < 2:\n        return None, None, \'none\'\n    \n    # Extract the relevant columns\n    x = df_group[\'max_storm_cat\'].values\n    y = df_group[\'duration\'].values\n    \n    # Calculate Pearson correlation\n    r, p_value = stats.pearsonr(x, y)\n    \n    # Determine relationship type\n    if p_value >= 0.05:\n        relationship_type = \'none\'\n    elif abs(r) >= 0.4:\n        relationship_type = \'linear\'\n    else:\n        relationship_type = \'nonlinear\'\n    \n    return r, p_value, relationship_type\n\n# Calculate for high damage group\nhigh_r, high_p, high_type = calculate_correlation_analysis(high_damage_df, \'high\')\nprint(f"High Damage - Correlation: r={high_r:.4f}, p={high_p:.4f}, Type: {high_type}")\n\n# Calculate for low damage group\nlow_r, low_p, low_type = calculate_correlation_analysis(low_damage_df, \'low\')\nprint(f"Low Damage - Correlation: r={low_r:.4f}, p={low_p:.4f}, Type: {low_type}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 6: Format Results and Create Final Answer Block\nNow I\'ll format the results according to the expected output format and create the FinalAnswerBlock.\n```\n```python\n# Round values according to specifications\nhigh_r_rounded = round(high_r, 2)\nhigh_p_rounded = round(high_p, 4)\nlow_r_rounded = round(low_r, 2)\nlow_p_rounded = round(low_p, 4)\n\nprint(f"\\nFormatted Results:")\nprint(f"High Damage - r: {high_r_rounded}, p: {high_p_rounded}, Type: {high_type}")\nprint(f"Low Damage - r: {low_r_rounded}, p: {low_p_rounded}, Type: {low_type}")\n\n# Create the final answer block\nfinal_answer_block = {\n    "final_answer_block": {\n        "question_id": 431,\n        "answers": {\n            "high_damage_correlation_coefficient": high_r_rounded,\n            "high_damage_p_value": high_p_rounded,\n            "high_damage_relationship_type": high_type,\n            "low_damage_correlation_coefficient": low_r_round