er to the required columns\nscaler = MinMaxScaler(feature_range=(0, 1))\ndf_scaled = df_imputed.copy()\n\n# Scale the required columns\ndf_scaled[required_columns] = scaler.fit_transform(df_imputed[required_columns])\n\n# Verify the scaling range\nprint("Scaled data statistics:")\nprint(df_scaled[required_columns].describe())\n\n# Check min and max values for each column\nprint("\\nMin values after scaling:")\nprint(df_scaled[required_columns].min())\nprint("\\nMax values after scaling:")\nprint(df_scaled[required_columns].max())\n```\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Perform distribution analysis using skewness and kurtosis to determine if the scaled variables follow a normal distribution.\n```\n```python\n# Calculate skewness and kurtosis for each scaled column\nskewness = df_scaled[required_columns].skew()\nkurtosis = df_scaled[required_columns].kurt()\n\nprint("Skewness values:")\nprint(skewness)\nprint("\\nKurtosis values:")\nprint(kurtosis)\n\n# Determine distribution type based on criteria\n# Normal if skewness is between -0.5 and 0.5 AND kurtosis is between -2 and 2\ndistribution_results = {}\nfor col in required_columns:\n    skew = skewness[col]\n    kurt = kurtosis[col]\n    is_normal = (-0.5 <= skew <= 0.5) and (-2 <= kurt <= 2)\n    distribution_results[col] = "Normal" if is_normal else "Non-Normal"\n\nprint("\\nDistribution type for each column:")\nfor col, dist_type in distribution_results.items():\n    print(f"{col}: {dist_type} (skewness={skewness[col]:.4f}, kurtosis={kurtosis[col]:.4f})")\n```\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Determine the overall distribution type and prepare the final answer.\n```\n```python\n# Determine overall distribution type\n# If all columns are normal, overall is "Normal", otherwise "Non-Normal"\nall_normal = all(dist_type == "Normal" for dist_type in distribution_results.values())\noverall_distribution = "Normal" if all_normal else "Non-Normal"\n\nprint(f"Overall distribution type: {overall_distribution}")\n\n# Prepare final answer va