ill still implement the median imputation step as specified in the requirements to ensure the preprocessing pipeline is complete. Then I\'ll apply MinMaxScaler to scale the variables to a range of 0-1.\n```\n```python\n# Step 1: Handle missing values using median imputation (if any)\nfor col in required_columns:\n    if df[col].isnull().sum() > 0:\n        df[col].fillna(df[col].median(), inplace=True)\n        print(f"Filled missing values in {col} with median: {df[col].median()}")\n\n# Verify no missing values remain\nremaining_missing = df[required_columns].isnull().sum().sum()\nprint(f"\\nRemaining missing values after imputation: {remaining_missing}")\n\n# Step 2: Apply MinMaxScaler to scale variables to range 0-1\nscaler = MinMaxScaler(feature_range=(0, 1))\nscaled_data = scaler.fit_transform(df[required_columns])\n\n# Create a DataFrame with scaled values\nscaled_df = pd.DataFrame(scaled_data, columns=required_columns, index=df.index)\n\nprint("\\nScaled data range:")\nprint(scaled_df.min())\nprint(scaled_df.max())\n```\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Perform distribution analysis using skewness and kurtosis to determine if the scaled variables follow a normal distribution\n```\n```markdown\nNow I\'ll calculate skewness and kurtosis for each scaled variable to determine the distribution type. According to the criteria:\n- If skewness is between -0.5 and 0.5 AND kurtosis is between -2 and 2, the distribution is considered Normal\n- Otherwise, it\'s Non-Normal\n```\n```python\n# Calculate skewness and kurtosis for each scaled variable\ndistribution_analysis = []\nall_normal = True\n\nfor col in required_columns:\n    skewness = stats.skew(scaled_df[col])\n    kurtosis = stats.kurtosis(scaled_df[col])\n    \n    # Check if distribution is normal based on criteria\n    is_normal = (-0.5 <= skewness <= 0.5) and (-2 <= kurtosis <= 2)\n    \n    distribution_analysis.append({\n        \'column\': col,\n        \'skewness\': skewness,\n        \'kurtosis\': kurtosis,\n        \'is_normal\': is_normal\n    })\n    \n    if not is_normal:\n        all_normal = False\n\n# Display distribution analysis results\nprint("Distribution Analysis Results:")\nprint("-" * 60)\nprint(f"{\'Column\':<20} {\'Skewness\':<15} {\'Kurtosis\':<15} {\'Normal?\'}")\nprint("-" * 60)\n\nfor row in distribution_analysis:\n    print(f"{row[\'column\']:<20} {row[\'skewness\']:<15.4f} {row[\'kurtosis\']:<15.4f} {\'Yes\' if row[\'is_normal\'] else \'No\'}")\n\nprint("-" * 60)\nprint(f"\\nOverall distribution type: {\'Normal\' if all_normal else \'Non-Normal\'}")\n```\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Generate the final answer in the required format\n```\n```markdown\nNow I\'ll compile the final results and format them according to the expected output format:\n- @missing_values_handled["Yes"/"No"]\n- @min_max_scaler_scale[range]\n- @distribution_type[distribution type]\n```\nTime consumed: 0.41s\nCode output:\nExit Code: 0\nOutput: \nDataset shape: (4177, 9)\n\nColumn names: [\'Sex\', \'Length\', \'Diameter\', \'Height\', \'Whole weight\', \'Shucked weight\', \'Viscera weight\', \'Shell weight\', \'Rings\']\n\nMissing values per column:\nSex               0\nLength            0\nDiameter          0\nHeight            0\nWhole weight      0\nShucked weight    0\nViscera weight    0\nShell weight      0\nRings             0\ndtype: int64\n\nData types:\nSex                object\nLength            float64\nDiameter          float64\nHeight            float64\nWhole weight      float64\nShucked weight    float64\nViscera weight    float64\nShell weight      float64\nRings               int64\ndtype: object\n\nTime consumed: 0.00s\nCode output:\nExit Code: 0\nOutput: \nMissing values in required columns:\nLength            0\nDiameter          0\nHeight            0\nWhole weight      0\nShucked weight    0\nViscera weight    0\nShell weight      0\ndtype: int64\n\nTotal missing values: 0\n\nMissing values handled: Yes\n\nTime consumed: 0.01s\nCode output:\nExit Code: 0\nOut