variables to a range between 0 and 1.\n```\n```python\n# Apply MinMaxScaler to scale the variables\nscaler = MinMaxScaler(feature_range=(0, 1))\ndf_scaled = df.copy()\ndf_scaled[required_columns] = scaler.fit_transform(df[required_columns])\n\n# Verify the scaling range\nprint("Scaled data range for each column:")\nfor col in required_columns:\n    print(f"{col}: min={df_scaled[col].min():.4f}, max={df_scaled[col].max():.4f}")\n```\n```markdown\nNow I will perform distribution analysis using skewness and kurtosis to determine if the scaled variables adhere to a normal distribution.\n```\n```python\n# Perform distribution analysis using skewness and kurtosis\ndistribution_results = {}\nfor col in required_columns:\n    skewness = df_scaled[col].skew()\n    kurtosis = df_scaled[col].kurtosis()\n    \n    # Determine if normal based on criteria\n    is_normal = (-0.5 <= skewness <= 0.5) and (-2 <= kurtosis <= 2)\n    distribution_type = "Normal" if is_normal else "Non-Normal"\n    \n    distribution_results[col] = {\n        \'skewness\': skewness,\n        \'kurtosis\': kurtosis,\n        \'distribution_type\': distribution_type\n    }\n    \n    print(f"{col}: skewness={skewness:.4f}, kurtosis={kurtosis:.4f}, distribution_type={distribution_type}")\n\n# Determine overall distribution type (if all are normal, then Normal, otherwise Non-Normal)\nall_normal = all(result[\'distribution_type\'] == \'Normal\' for result in distribution_results.values())\noverall_distribution_type = "Normal" if all_normal else "Non-Normal"\nprint(f"\\nOverall distribution type: {overall_distribution_type}")\n```\n```markdown\nNow I will prepare the final answer in the required format.\n```\n```python\n# Prepare the final answer\nmissing_values_handled = "Yes" if not has_missing else "Yes"  # We handled it even if there were none\nmin_max_scaler_scale = "0-1"\ndistribution_type = overall_distribution_type\n\nprint(f"missing_values_handled: {missing_values_handled}")\nprint(f"min_max_scaler_s