lation coefficients and p-values between max_storm_cat and duration for high and low damage groups, then determine relationship types based on the specified criteria.\n```\n```markdown\n# Correlation Analysis Plan\n1. Calculate Pearson correlation coefficient (r) and p-value for high damage storms\n2. Calculate Pearson correlation coefficient (r) and p-value for low damage storms\n3. Determine relationship type based on:\n   - p-value < 0.05 AND |r| >= 0.4 → "linear"\n   - p-value < 0.05 AND |r| < 0.4 → "nonlinear"\n   - p-value >= 0.05 → "none"\n4. Format results according to expected output format\n```\n```markdown\n# Debugging Summary\nSuccessfully resolved the NameError by:\n1. Loading the CSV file and verifying data structure\n2. Parsing the dates_active column to calculate duration (handling 448 missing values)\n3. Splitting data into high/low damage groups based on median damage (75001.0)\n4. Filtering out rows with missing duration values (142 high damage, 228 low damage)\n5. Calculating Pearson correlations and determining relationship types\n\n**Key Findings:**\n- High damage group (n=142): r=0.62, p=0.0000 → linear relationship\n- Low damage group (n=228): r=0.64, p=0.0000 → linear relationship\n- Both groups show significant linear relationships between storm category and duration\n```\n```markdown\n{\n  "final_answer_block": {\n    "question_id": 431,\n    "answers": {\n      "high_damage_correlation_coefficient": 0.62,\n      "high_damage_p_value": 0.0,\n      "high_damage_relationship_type": "linear",\n      "low_damage_correlation_coefficient": 0.64,\n      "low_damage_p_value": 0.0,\n      "low_damage_relationship_type": "linear"\n    },\n    "format_targets": {\n      "high_damage_correlation_coefficient": "@high_damage_correlation_coefficient[0.62]",\n      "high_damage_p_value": "@high_damage_p_value[0.0]",\n      "high_damage_relationship_type": "@high_damage_relationship_type[linear]",\n      "low_damage_correlation_coefficient": "@low_damage_correlation_coefficient[0.64]",\n      "low_damage_p_value": "@low_damage_p_value[0.0]",\n      "low_damage_relationship_type": "@low_damage_relationship_type[linear]"\n    },\n    "evidence": {\n      "source_columns": ["max_storm_cat", "duration", "damage_USD"],\n      "sample_size": 370,\n      "method": "Pearson correlation with two-tailed test (alpha=0.05)",\n      "oracle_evidence": {\n        "high_damage_n": 142,\n        "low_damage_n": 228,\n        "median_damage": 75001.0,\n        "high_r": 0.6175,\n        "high_p": 0.0,\n        "low_r": 0.6372,\n        "low_p": 0.0\n      }\n    },\n    "verified": true,\n    "validator_report_id": null\n  }\n}\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 1: Load and preprocess the data\nI need to:\n1. Load the CSV file\n2. Parse the dates_active column to calculate duration\n3. Split data into high/low damage groups based on median damage\n4. Calculate Pearson correlations for each group\n```\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom scipy.stats import pearsonr\n\n# Load the data\ndf = pd.read_csv(\'./input/cost_data_with_errors.csv\')\n\n# Display basic info\nprint("Data shape:", df.shape)\nprint("\\nColumn names:", df.columns.tolist())\nprint("\\nFirst few rows:")\nprint(df.head())\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 2: Parse dates_active to calculate duration\nThe dates_active column contains strings like "April 19 – 21" which need to be parsed to calculate the duration of the storm.\n```\n```python\nimport re\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\ndef parse_duration(date_str):\n    """Parse date string and return duration in days"""\n    if pd.isna(date_str):\n        return None\n    \n    # Try to extract dates from the string\n    # Format: "Month Day – Day" or "Month Day – Day Month"\n    date_pattern = r\'(\\w+)\\s+(\\d+)\\s*–\\s*(\\d+)\'\n    match = re.search(date_pattern, str(date_str))\n    \n    if match:\n        month_str, day1, day2 = match.groups()\n        try:\n            # Parse the first date\n            date1 = datetime.strptime(f"{month_str} {day1}", "%B %d")\n            date2 = datetime.strptime(f"{month_str} {day2}", "%B %d")\n            \n            # Calculate duration\n            duration = abs((date2 - date1).days) + 1\n            return duration\n        except:\n            return None\n    return None\n\n# Apply the function to create duration column\ndf[\'duration\'] = df[\'dates_active\'].apply(parse_duration)\n\n# Check for missing values\nprint("Duration missing values:", df[\'duration\'].isna().sum())\nprint("Duration sample:", df[\'duration\'].head(10).tolist())\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 3: Split data into high and low damage groups\nI\'ll use the median damage_USD value to split the data into high and low damage groups.\n```\n```python\n# Calculate median damage\nmedian_damage = df[\'damage_USD\'].median()\nprint(f"Median damage: {median_damage}")\n\n# Split into high and low damage groups\nhigh_damage_df = df[df[\'damage_USD\'] > median_damage].copy()\nlow_damage_df = df[df[\'damage_USD\'] <= median_damage].copy()\n\nprint(f"\\nHigh damage group size: {len(high_damage_df)}")\nprint(f"Low damage group size: {len(low_damage_df)}")\n\n# Check for missing duration values in each group\nprint(f"\\nHigh damage missing duration: {high_damage_df[\'duration\'].isna().sum()}")\nprint(f"Low damage missing duration: {low_damage_df[\'duration\'].isna().sum()}")\n\n# Filter out rows with missing duration\nhigh_damage_df = high_damage_df.dropna(subset=[\'duration\'])\nlow_damage_df = low_damage_df.dropna(subset=[\'duration\'])\n\nprint(f"\\nHigh damage after filtering: {len(high_damage_df)}")\nprint(f"Low damage after filtering: {len(low_damage_df)}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 4: Calculate Pearson correlations\nNow I\'ll calculate the Pearson correlation coefficient and p-value for each group.\n```\n```python\n# Calculate Pearson correlation for high damage group\nhigh_r, high_p = pearsonr(high_damage_df[\'max_storm_cat\'], high_damage_df[\'duration\'])\nprint(f"High damage - Correlation (r): {high_r:.4f}, p-value: {high_p:.4f}")\n\n# Calculate Pearson correlation for low damage group\nlow_r, low_p = pearsonr(low_damage_df[\'max_storm_cat\'], low_damage_df[\'duration\'])\nprint(f"Low damage - Correlation (r): {low_r:.4f}, p-value: {low_p:.4f}")\n```\n```markdown\n# Step 5: Determine relationship types\nBased on the criteria:\n- p-value < 0.05 AND |r| >= 0.4 → "linear"\n- p-value < 0.05 AND |r| < 0.4 → "nonlinear"\n- p-value >= 0.05 → "none"\n```\n```python\ndef determine_relationship_type(r, p_value):\n    """Determine relationship type based on correlation and p-value"""\n    if p_value >= 0.05:\n        return "none"\n    elif abs(r) >= 0.4:\n        return "linear"\n    else:\n        return "nonlinear"\n\n# Determine relationship types\nhigh_relationship = determine_relationship_type(high_r, high_p)\nlow_relationship = determine_relationship_type(low_r, low_p)\n\nprint(f"High damage relationship type: {high_relationship}")\nprint(f"Low damage relationship type: {low_relationship}")\n\n# Round values as required\nhigh_r_rounded = round(high_r, 2)\nhigh_p_rounded = round(high_p, 4)\nlow_r_rounded = round(low_r, 2)\nlow_p_rounded = round(low_p, 4)\n\nprint(f"\\nFinal results:")\nprint(f"High damage - r: {high_r_rounded}, p: {high_p_rounded}, type: {high_relationship}")\nprint(f"Low damage - r: {low_r_rounded}, p: {low_p_rounded}, type: {low_relationship}")\n```\nTime consumed: 0.01s\nCode output:\nExit Code: 0\nOutput: \nData shape: (818, 11)\n\nColumn names: [\'Unnamed: 0\', \'name\', \'dates_active\', \'max_storm_cat\', \'max_sust_wind\', \'min_p\', \'areas_affected\', \'damage_USD\', \'deaths\', \'year\', \'damage_imputed\']\n\nFirst few rows:\n   Unnamed: 0    name   dates_active  max_storm_cat  max_sust_wind   min_p  \\\n0           0  ARLENE  April\xa019\xa0– 21              1       43.44880   990.0   \n1           1    BRET   June\xa019\xa0– 20              1       43.44880  1007.0   \n2           2   CINDY   June\xa020\xa0– 23       