ly MinMaxScaler to scale the variables to range 0-1\n```\n```python\n# Apply median imputation for missing values\nfor col in required_columns:\n    if df[col].isnull().any():\n        df[col].fillna(df[col].median(), inplace=True)\n\n# Verify no missing values remain\nmissing_after_imputation = df[required_columns].isnull().sum().sum()\nprint(f"Missing values after imputation: {missing_after_imputation}")\n\n# Apply MinMaxScaler\nscaler = MinMaxScaler(feature_range=(0, 1))\ndf_scaled = df.copy()\ndf_scaled[required_columns] = scaler.fit_transform(df[required_columns])\n\n# Verify the scaling range\nprint("\\nScaled data range for each column:")\nfor col in required_columns:\n    print(f"{col}: min={df_scaled[col].min():.4f}, max={df_scaled[col].max():.4f}")\n```\n```markdown\n[STEP GOAL]: Perform distribution analysis using skewness and kurtosis to determine if the scaled variables follow a normal distribution.\n```\n```markdown\n# Distribution Analysis\nI will calculate skewness and kurtosis for each scaled variable and determine if they follow a normal distribution based on the criteria:\n- Skewness between -0.5 and 0.5\n- Kurtosis between -2 and 2\n```\n```python\n# Calculate skewness and kurtosis for each scaled column\ndistribution_results = {}\nall_normal = True\n\nfor col in required_columns:\n    skewness = df_scaled[col].skew()\n    kurtosis = df_scaled[col].kurtosis()\n    \n    # Check if normal based on criteria\n    is_normal = (-0.5 <= skewness <= 0.5) and (-2 <= kurtosis <= 2)\n    \n    distribution_results[col] = {\n        \'skewness\': skewness,\n        \'kurtosis\': kurtosis,\n        \'is_normal\': is_normal\n    }\n    \n    if not is_normal:\n        all_normal = False\n    \n    print(f"{col}: skewness={skewness:.4f}, kurtosis={kurtosis:.4f}, normal={is_normal}")\n\n# Determine overall distribution type\ndistribution_type = "Normal" if all_normal else "Non-Normal"\nprint(f"\\nOverall distribution type: {distribution_type}")\n```\n```markdow